# Sourced, not executed. Points the application at the database in THIS
# container, and is shared by every service that opens a connection.
#
# Why it exists at all: `config.py` defaults POSTGRES_HOST to `db`, which is the
# right answer for docker-compose.yml and meaningless here. Somebody deploying
# this will bring the .env they already have, and that file says `db` — so
# without this the app starts, reports healthy (liveness is deliberately
# database-free) and then fails on the first real request with a name it cannot
# resolve. This project has already lost an evening to that exact symptom from a
# different cause; see the note about `7CtV@db` in CLAUDE.md.
#
# It is one file because two copies of it is one copy that gets forgotten — and
# it *was* forgotten: the first boot of this image brought Postgres up correctly
# and then failed in `migrate`, because only the api service had this logic.
#
# DATABASE_URL remains the documented escape hatch for "a database the
# POSTGRES_* fields cannot describe". Set it and this steps aside entirely, which
# is how you point this image at a Postgres you run elsewhere.
if [ -z "${DATABASE_URL:-}" ]; then
    export POSTGRES_HOST=127.0.0.1
    export POSTGRES_PORT=5432
fi
